description:
A preorder traversal lists a node before its two subtrees; an inorder traversal lists the left subtree, then the node, then the right subtree. Given both traversals of the same tree, and with every value distinct, exactly one tree fits. Rebuild it.

The input is three lines:

1. an integer `n`, the number of nodes;
2. the `n` preorder values, separated by spaces;
3. the `n` inorder values, separated by spaces.

Print the resulting tree on one line using the very same level-order encoding: values and `null` tokens separated by single spaces, with trailing `null` tokens dropped.

An inorder traversal lists the left subtree, then the node, then the right subtree; a postorder traversal lists both subtrees before the node. Given both traversals of the same tree, and with every value distinct, exactly one tree fits. Rebuild it.

The input is three lines:

1. an integer `n`, the number of nodes;
2. the `n` inorder values, separated by spaces;
3. the `n` postorder values, separated by spaces.

Print the resulting tree on one line using the very same level-order encoding: values and `null` tokens separated by single spaces, with trailing `null` tokens dropped.
